From 8c5fb27b67971d80225a38c1bd2664bdd402b06c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 13:29:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] DHCP: a request nobody answers is not an answer of "no"

An interface that has held a lease starts every negotiation in INIT_REBOOT,
asking for the address it remembers. If that request is answered by a NAK the
client goes back to INIT and broadcasts a DISCOVER, which is right. If it is
answered by nothing at all, it gave up -- and since _CurrentState() returns
INIT_REBOOT for as long as an address is remembered and the last attempt
failed, every later attempt asks the same unanswered question again.

RFC 2131 4.4.2 says a client that gets no response to its REQUEST should
restart the initialisation procedure. Do that: forget the address and go to
INIT, where a client that had never been configured starts. SELECTING still
gives up, so a network with no server at all does not spin.

This is a correctness fix against the RFC, not a fix for a bug seen in the
wild: it was written from a misread packet capture -- tcpdump's "Request" is
the BOOTP op field, which every client message carries, not DHCPREQUEST. The
capture that prompted it in fact showed the client broadcasting DISCOVER
correctly and nothing on the network answering, which this does not address.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Prose-Patch: 0120-dhcp-a-request-nobody-answers-is-not-an-answer-of-no.patch 1ebcbbd1aa0224703f44afff2c7f0f69f2f8ea66482a1edb30d702dac577e538
---
 src/servers/net/DHCPClient.cpp | 15 ++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/servers/net/DHCPClient.cpp b/src/servers/net/DHCPClient.cpp
index c1e0e97b28..5ff999f627 100644
--- a/src/servers/net/DHCPClient.cpp
+++ b/src/servers/net/DHCPClient.cpp
@@ -627,7 +627,20 @@ DHCPClient::_Negotiate()
 	status_t status = B_OK;
 	while (state != BOUND && fNegotiateThread != -1) {
 		status = _StateTransition(socket, state);
-		if (status != B_OK && (state == SELECTING || state == REBOOTING))
+		if (status != B_OK && state == REBOOTING) {
+			// RFC 2131 4.4.2: a REQUEST for the address we remember that is
+			// answered by nothing at all is not an answer of "no". Giving up
+			// here left an interface that had once held a lease asking for it
+			// again every time, for ever, and never once broadcasting the
+			// DISCOVER that would have got it a different one -- a server that
+			// has forgotten the binding, or stopped answering an expired one,
+			// strands the interface permanently. Start again from the top,
+			// which is what a client that had never been configured does.
+			fAssignedAddress = 0;
+			state = INIT;
+			continue;
+		}
+		if (status != B_OK && state == SELECTING)
 			break;
 	}
 
